the building block skill in trigonometry  is being able to talk about the  different sides of a triangle in  relation to each other so that's why  we're looking at identifying sides in  triangles so here's a right angle  triangle we start by calling this angle  over here theta which is a greek letter  and i want to name each of these three  sides in relation to theta okay now  because it's a right angle triangle uh  you've got two shorter signs and then  this longest side which is always  opposite the right angle and as we know  that longer side has this special name  the hypotenuse which we learned in  pythagoras theorem okay so that one's  always the hypotenuse no matter which  angle you're looking at because it's a  unique sign but these other two signs  how shall i name them well i can see  that this side down the bottom  well it's next to the angle theta next  to another word for that is adjacent so  we call this the adjacent side and then  you've got this other one which all the  way on the other side it's opposite it's  facing  theta okay so you can see it's looking  all the way across so you've got the  hypotenuse which is always the longest  side that's always opposite the right  angle you've got the adjacent side down  here and then you've got the opposite  side all the way across the other side  of the triangle from theta okay now the  important thing to get is that even  though the hypotenuse stays the same  whichever angle you're looking at  the opposite and adjacent sides they  will switch around depending on your  point of view so if i draw the same  triangle again  okay  here we go and i've got my right angle  which means that this side over here is  the hypotenuse  so now if i look from up here okay uh if  i this is a different angle so i'm going  to call it alpha  which is another greek letter  uh now the hypotenuse is still the  hypotenuse it's still the longest side  okay but my opposite and my adjacent  sides have switched over you can see  that  this side up here the vertical one  that's the one that's next to or  adjacent to alpha so now it's the  adjacent side and of course if i look  all the way across from the other side  the opposite side is down here now  so  the tricky thing to get is that your  adjacent and your opposite side they  change depending on your point of view  and that happens all the time in real  life okay so we want to think about well  which perspective are we looking from  okay  now  we want to be able to talk about how all  of these sides relate to each other and  that's what the trigonometric ratios are  about  after which trigonometry is net so  the trigonometric ratios  or it's often shortened to  trig ratios  there's three of them  which match up to the relationships  between the sides okay so let's redraw  this guy  so if i got theta down here that means  i've got the opposite which i'm going to  abbreviate to opp the adjacent side  which i'll abbreviate like this and the  hypotenuse okay so i've got my right  angle here  so the three ratios are they're called  sine  cosine  and  tangent and it's a bit long so we  abbreviate them we call this one well  sine is just one syllable but we write  it without the e on the end we call this  one cos and we abbreviate this one to  tan  okay so the way it works is that it's a  ratio each one is a ratio between a pair  of sides so i would write it as sine of  this angle theta  is equal to the opposite side on the  hypotenuse opposite side on  hypotenuse that's sine cos  is the adjacent side on the hypotenuse  and the last one is tan  which is the opposite side this one on  the adjacent side that's the two shorter  sides  okay and you might notice as well that's  actually also equal to sine theta on cos  theta because if i did this divided by  this the hypotenuse these denominators  would cancel you'd just be left left  with these numerators opposite on  adjacent  okay which gives you the same ratio so  um a mnemonic device uh from remembering  uh all of this because it's quite hard  is to notice that you've got uh if you  like that's o over  h a over h and this one is o over a so s  goes with o h so people write s o h sine  is opposite of hypotenuse  cos goes with a and h  cosine equals adjacent on hypotenuse and  t goes with o and a  tan  equals opposite on adjacent so we read  that as sokka toa which is a bit  long and awkward and not a real word but  it does help us to remember what all the  ratios are
 

